@node Introduction, Installing Packages, Copying, Top
@chapter Introduction

The ELPA client lets XEmacs consume packages published in the GNU ELPA
format, from GNU ELPA and from MELPA: it downloads an archive index,
resolves a package by name, installs its files into a versioned content
directory under @file{~/.emacs.d/elpa/}, generates autoloads,
byte-compiles the source, and activates the package by adding it to
@code{load-path} and loading its autoloads.  After activation the
package works like any other Lisp library on the system.

The client is deliberately minimal.  It supports @emph{installing} a
small, explicitly allowed set of packages.  It consumes prebuilt
tarballs (and GNU ELPA's single-file artifacts) only: it never builds
from MELPA recipes or package source repositories.  It does not provide
a package menu, browsing, deletion, rollback, or signature
verification.  The old XEmacs package system (@file{package-get.el} and
friends) is unrelated: it has its own index format and download path,
and the two coexist without interacting.

The milestone-1 target set is nine dependency-free MELPA packages plus
the two GNU ELPA packages documented below.  All nine MELPA targets
install and run from unmodified upstream tarballs with no new
compatibility code; this is the @dfn{zero-shim} policy.

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .75
@headitem Package @tab Role
@item @code{loop} @tab control flow
@item @code{levenshtein} @tab edit distance
@item @code{csv} @tab CSV parsing
@item @code{vector-utils} @tab vector manipulation
@item @code{number} @tab number parsing and formatting
@item @code{rings} @tab buffer rings
@item @code{shell-split-string} @tab shell-like string splitting
@item @code{kv} @tab key/value data structures
@item @code{unidecode} @tab Unicode transliteration; declares
@code{lexical-binding: t} yet runs unchanged
@end multitable

@code{queue} (a @code{tar} package) and @code{loc-changes} (a
@code{single}-file package) exercise the GNU ELPA path.  All
milestone-1 targets install and run in a fresh XEmacs with no
dependencies beyond core.

Milestone 2 adds four MELPA targets that require the compatibility
layer (@pxref{Compatibility Layer}):

@multitable @columnfractions .20 .24 .26 .30
@headitem Package @tab Pinned version @tab Requirement @tab Role
@item @code{dmacro} @tab @code{20241027.830} @tab @code{cl-lib} 0.6; @code{emacs} ignored @tab array search
@item @code{checkbox} @tab @code{20141117.58} @tab @code{cl-lib} 0.5; @code{emacs} ignored @tab list checkbox toggle
@item @code{es-lib} @tab @code{20141111.1830} @tab @code{cl-lib} 0.3 @tab string property removal
@item @code{buffer-sets} @tab @code{20260817.2118} @tab @code{cl-lib} 0.5 @tab buffer-set membership
@end multitable

The milestone-2 targets install and run from unmodified upstream
tarballs at their pinned date versions.  GNU ELPA @code{queue} 0.2 is
the GNU-path fixture and live artifact, not a promised target.
Synthetic packages witness cross-archive resolution in the offline
tests; they are never defaults in the allowlist.

Target sets are support contracts, not user-facing restrictions:
users may extend the allowlist (@pxref{Customization} and
@pxref{Security}).

@node Installing Packages, Customization, Introduction, Top
@chapter Installing Packages

@menu
* Refreshing the Index::  Fetching what the archive offers.
* Installing a Package::  Download, build, and activate.
* Activation at Startup:: Packages that load themselves.
@end menu

MELPA is not configured by default.  To install MELPA's target set,
add a MELPA archive to @code{elpa-archives}:

@example
(setq elpa-archives
      '(("melpa" . "https://melpa.org/packages/")
        ("gnu" . "https://elpa.gnu.org/packages/")))
@end example

@noindent
@code{elpa-install} reads the first configured archive, so list the
archive you install from first; the remaining entries are available to
@code{elpa-refresh-contents}, @code{elpa-package-info}, and dependency
resolution.  Configuring
an archive only selects a source: the allowlist still governs what may
be installed, and a package outside the milestone target set must be
added to @code{elpa-installable-packages} first
(@pxref{Customization}).

@node Refreshing the Index
@section Refreshing the Index

An @dfn{archive} publishes an index file @file{archive-contents}
listing everything it offers.  @code{elpa-install} refreshes the
selected archive's index before resolving; you can also refresh it
explicitly:

@deffn Command elpa-refresh-contents &optional archive
Fetch, validate, cache, and parse the index of @var{archive}, an
archive name from @code{elpa-archives}; the first configured archive
when omitted.  Return the number of packages the index lists.  The
index must carry archive version 1; anything else is rejected.  A local
directory location is read as a file; an HTTP(S) location is fetched
over the network.  @code{elpa-install} performs this refresh itself
before resolving, so an explicit refresh is only needed to inspect an
index or to force a cached copy up to date.
@end deffn

@node Installing a Package
@section Installing a Package

@deffn Command elpa-install name &optional version
Install the package @var{name} (a symbol or string) from the first
archive in @code{elpa-archives}.  The name must appear in
@code{elpa-installable-packages}; anything else is refused.

Install always refreshes the archive index before resolving.  With no
@var{version}, this is a @dfn{floating install}: the client installs
whatever build the archive currently advertises, which is what a
rolling-release archive such as MELPA requires.  A cached index is
never trusted for a rolling archive, because old artifacts disappear
and a stale cache can point at a dead download.  When @var{version} is
given, the archive must still advertise exactly that version; a version
the archive has pruned signals that the version is no longer available
in that archive.  Returns @var{name} on success.

Installing a newer build replaces the older one in place.  The client
publishes the new content directory and only then deletes the old
build, so a failed replacement leaves the old build intact, and after a
successful one exactly one active content directory remains for the
package.  Installation of each package is all-or-nothing: the download
lands in a staging directory and the content directory is published by
an atomic rename only after validation, autoload generation, and
byte-compilation have all succeeded; a failure removes that staging
residue and leaves no partial content directory.  Requirements install
first, resolved across every configured archive
(@pxref{Cross-Archive Dependencies}); a failure can leave dependencies
that completed before it in place.
@end deffn

@defun elpa-installed-p name &optional version
Return non-@code{nil} if the content directory for @var{name} (at
@var{version}, when given) exists under @code{elpa-user-dir}.  Without
@var{version}, the query keys off the newest installed build, not the
first directory encountered, so it reflects what actually runs.
@end defun

@defun elpa-package-info name &optional archive
Return the plist describing @var{name} as its archive advertises it,
with properties @code{:name}, @code{:version}, @code{:reqs},
@code{:summary}, @code{:kind} (@code{tar} or @code{single}), and
@code{:archive}.
@end defun

@node Activation at Startup
@section Activation at Startup

@deffn Command elpa-activate-all
Add every installed package's content directory to @code{load-path}
and load its generated autoloads file.  Idempotent: directories and
autoloads already activated are skipped.  Entries that are not package
directories (archives, staging) are ignored.
@end deffn

At startup XEmacs calls @code{elpa-activate-all}
automatically when @code{elpa-user-dir} exists and
@code{elpa-activate-at-startup} is non-@code{nil}, so packages installed
with autoload cookies are callable without an explicit
@code{require}.

@node Customization, Security, Installing Packages, Top
@chapter Customization

@defopt elpa-archives
The archives the client knows, as an alist of
@code{(@var{name} . @var{location})}.  The default is GNU ELPA only:
@code{(("gnu" . "https://elpa.gnu.org/packages/"))}.  MELPA is
supported but not configured by default; add an entry such as
@code{("melpa" . "https://melpa.org/packages/")} to use it.  A location
is either an @code{http}s URL or a local directory path (useful for
testing and air-gapped installs).  Archive configuration selects a
source; it does not grant trust (@pxref{Security}).
@end defopt

@defopt elpa-user-dir
The directory packages are installed into,
@file{~/.emacs.d/elpa/} by default.  Created on demand.  Package
content lives in @code{<name>-<version>/} subdirectories; the cached
archive indexes live in @file{archives/@var{name}/}; downloads stage
in @file{.staging/}.
@end defopt

@defopt elpa-installable-packages
The allowlist of packages the client may install, @code{(queue
loc-changes dmacro checkbox es-lib buffer-sets loop levenshtein csv
vector-utils number rings shell-split-string kv unidecode)} by default:
the thirteen MELPA targets from milestones 1 and 2, plus the two GNU
ELPA targets.  Every dependency name must also appear here
(@pxref{Dependency Allowlist}).  This list, not the packages' own
declared compatibility, is the safety boundary: a name not on the list
is refused before any download happens.  Trust is by name and spans
all configured archives.  See @ref{Security}.
@end defopt

@defopt elpa-activate-at-startup
Whether startup runs @code{elpa-activate-all} automatically.  Non-nil
by default; set @code{nil} to keep installed packages inert until
@code{elpa-activate-all} is called explicitly.
@end defopt

@node Security, Data Model, Customization, Top
@chapter Security

This client sits between two kinds of archive.  MELPA is an
@dfn{unsigned-by-design archive}: it publishes no signatures for its
index or its artifacts, as a property of the archive, so allowing it is
a posture rather than a deferred feature.  GNU ELPA is a
@dfn{verification-deferred archive}: it signs its index and packages,
but this client does not perform those checks yet.

The @emph{only} trust controls are HTTPS transport and the flat
per-package allowlist in @code{elpa-installable-packages}.  There is no
signature verification, no checksum pinning, and no TLS pinning.  The
allowlist grants trust by name across all configured archives; it is
not a per-archive permission, because trust and source selection are
different concepts.  Dependency resolution is not a second trust
channel: every dependency name that is not core-provided (and is not
the @code{emacs} pseudo-dependency), at every depth, must already be on
the allowlist (@pxref{Dependency Allowlist}).  The MELPA grant is inert
until a MELPA archive is configured.  The @code{emacs}
pseudo-dependency is ignored with a warning, since XEmacs cannot
truthfully claim the declared Emacs version.

The residual risk is stated verbatim in the client header:

@quotation
Trust model: this client performs no signature verification. MELPA
publishes no signatures, by design; GNU ELPA signature checking is
deferred to a future core effort. The only controls are HTTPS transport
and the `elpa-installable-packages' allowlist: if an archive is
compromised or the connection is tampered with, any allowlisted package
can run arbitrary code. The allowlist is not a cast-iron guarantee —
keep it short, and install only packages you would trust with your
keystrokes.
@end quotation

The archive-shape checks described under @ref{Data Model} are failure
containment, not authentication: a @code{tar} package is validated
before extraction (every member must live under the single top-level
@code{<name>-<version>/} directory; absolute paths, @code{..}
components, and link entries are rejected before @command{tar} runs),
and autoloads files shipped inside a tarball are deleted, never loaded.
They cannot detect a malicious package that bears an allowlisted name.

@node Data Model, Lisp API, Security, Top
@chapter Data Model

@menu
* Archive Index::       The archive-contents format.
* Install Layout::      What ends up on disk.
@end menu

@node Archive Index
@section Archive Index

An index is one Lisp form read from @file{archive-contents}.  The first
element is the integer @code{1}, the archive format version; any other
value is an error.  Each following entry has the shape

@example
(@var{name} . [(@var{maj} @var{min} @dots{}) @var{requirements}
               @var{summary} @var{kind} @var{extras}])
@end example

@noindent
where @var{version} is a version list, @var{requirements} is
@code{nil} or @code{((@var{dep} (@var{maj} @var{min}) @dots{}) @dots{})},
@var{summary} is a string, @var{kind} is @code{tar} or @code{single},
and @var{extras} (URLs, maintainers, commit hashes) is ignored.

Date versions such as MELPA's @code{20240331.1300} need no separate
version type: @code{version-to-list} parses one as @code{(20240331
1300)}, and @code{elpa-version-join} renders it back byte-identically,
so it is safe as an artifact and directory name.  MELPA entries also
carry @code{:commit} and @code{:revdesc} extras; like all extras they
are ignored.  MELPA publishes no @code{single} artifacts and no
prerelease markers.

@node Install Layout
@section Install Layout

@example
~/.emacs.d/elpa/
archives/gnu/archive-contents        @r{cached GNU ELPA index}
archives/melpa/archive-contents      @r{cached MELPA index}
queue-0.2/queue.el                   @r{package source}
queue-0.2/queue.elc                  @r{byte-compiled}
queue-0.2/queue-pkg.el               @r{package metadata (tar only)}
queue-0.2/queue-autoloads.el         @r{generated}
queue-0.2/queue-autoloads.elc
levenshtein-20090830.1040/           @r{date-versioned MELPA package}
.staging/                            @r{transient; removed after install}
@end example

A @code{single} package installs the same way minus @file{-pkg.el}: its
downloaded @file{<name>-<version>.el} is written into the content
directory as @file{<name>.el}.

There is exactly one content directory per package.  Replacing an older
build publishes the new @code{<name>-<version>/} directory first and
deletes the old one only after the publish succeeds; a failure leaves
the old build in place.  There is no rollback or retention policy.

@node Lisp API, Compatibility Layer, Data Model, Top
@chapter Lisp API

This chapter is the reference for the public functions and for the
compatibility helpers the client ships.  Internal functions
(@code{elpa--}@tie{}prefix) are not documented here.

@menu
* Versions::              Parsing and joining version lists.
* Compatibility Shims::   wholenump and redisplay.
@end menu

@node Versions
@section Versions

Versions are handled as GNU-style version @dfn{lists}:
@code{"0.2"} is @code{(0 2)}.  Comparisons are list comparisons, never
string comparisons.

The client ports GNU's version machinery into core @file{subr.el}:

@defun version-to-list version
Parse the version string @var{version} into a list of integers.
Understands numeric components and the prerelease markers @code{pre},
@code{beta}, @code{alpha}, and @code{snapshot}, ordered as in GNU
package.el.
@end defun

@defun version-list-< l1 l2
@defunx version-list-<= l1 l2
@defunx version-list-= l1 l2
Compare version lists @var{l1} and @var{l2}.
@end defun

@defun elpa-version-join version-list
The inverse: render @var{version-list} as a dotted string,
@code{(0 2)} @result{} @code{"0.2"}.  Used for directory and artifact
names.
@end defun

@node Compatibility Shims
@section Compatibility Shims

Two small definitions let the target packages run under XEmacs.  Both
are defined only when absent, so a future core implementation wins:

@defun wholenump object
Alias for @code{natnump}: non-@code{nil} when @var{object} is a
non-negative integer.
@end defun

@defun redisplay &rest args
Forces a redisplay via @code{(sit-for 0)}.  Note this replaces the
redisplay primitive's usual role for callers that invoke it on
interactive paths; in batch mode it is harmless.
@end defun

@code{line-number-at-pos} already exists in core; it is deliberately
@emph{not} redefined.

@node Compatibility Layer, Cross-Archive Dependencies, Lisp API, Top
@chapter Compatibility Layer

The compatibility layer is core, not a package: it has no package
version and is not installed from an archive.  It supplies the
GNU-only APIs the milestone-2 targets need.  The @dfn{support contract}
is the subset of those APIs this manual guarantees; anything outside
it is unsupported even if a vendored file happens to define it.

@menu
* Support Contract::      The symbols the targets exercise.
* Dumped Macros::        when-let, if-let, and friends.
* Excluded APIs::        What this milestone does not provide.
@end menu

@node Support Contract, Dumped Macros, Compatibility Layer, Compatibility Layer
@section Support Contract

A symbol is supported when a target compiles, loads, and exercises it,
not merely because it is present.  The contract is:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{cl-lib}: @code{cl-defun}, @code{cl-defmacro},
@code{cl-destructuring-bind}, @code{cl-loop} (which expands through
@code{cl-block} and @code{cl-return-from}), @code{cl-letf},
@code{cl-case}, @code{cl-incf}, @code{cl-dotimes}, @code{cl-macrolet},
@code{cl-assert}, @code{cl-position} (patched), @code{cl-rest},
@code{cl-member}, and @code{cl-find-if}.
@item
Dumped @file{subr.el}: @code{when-let}, @code{if-let},
@code{defvar-local}, and @code{with-eval-after-load}.
@item
Dumped @file{files.el} and @file{easy-mmode.el}:
@code{locate-user-emacs-file}, which @code{buffer-sets} evaluates at
load time, and @code{define-globalized-minor-mode}, which
@code{dmacro} calls at load time (it is an alias for
@code{easy-mmode-define-global-mode}).
@item
Existing core: @code{derived}, @code{font-lock}, @code{apropos},
@code{loadhist}, and @code{cl} (which satisfies @code{queue}'s
compile-time require).
@item
Key parsing: @code{kbd} and @code{read-kbd-macro}, which
@code{buffer-sets} uses at load time.
@end itemize

The vendored @code{cl-lib} is GNU ELPA 0.7.1, XEmacs-adapted and
delivered from @file{lisp/}.  Only the symbols above are guaranteed.
The adaptation's @code{defadvice} is a load-time no-op stub that
implements no advice, and the direct @code{cl-position} definition is
what @code{checkbox} exercises.  @code{cl-flet}, @code{cl-flet*}, and
@code{cl-delete-duplicates} are not promised.

@node Dumped Macros, Excluded APIs, Support Contract, Compatibility Layer
@section Dumped Macros

@code{when-let}, @code{if-let}, @code{defvar-local}, and
@code{with-eval-after-load} are defined in the dumped
@file{subr.el}, so they are available at compile and load time with no
@code{require}.  This matters because the targets call @code{when-let}
and @code{if-let} without requiring anything.

@code{defvar-local} and @code{with-eval-after-load} are in
@file{subr.el} upstream.  @code{when-let} and @code{if-let} are
upstream @file{subr-x.el} macros; dumping them in @file{subr.el} is a
deliberate divergence, because a lazily loaded @file{subr-x} library
could not satisfy a caller that never requires it.  This milestone has
no @code{subr-x} feature.

@node Excluded APIs, , Dumped Macros, Compatibility Layer
@section Excluded APIs

None of the following is provided or guaranteed:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{version<}.
@item
A @code{subr-x} feature.
@item
@code{overlay}.
@item
The GNU core libraries @code{xml}, @code{color}, @code{help-mode}, and
@code{smie}.
@item
Org and BibTeX support.
@item
Lexical binding, including interpreter-only lexical binding.
@end itemize

Anything outside the support contract is unsupported, even if it
happens to work.

@node Cross-Archive Dependencies, Install Pipeline, Compatibility Layer, Top
@chapter Cross-Archive Dependencies

A package's requirements are resolved across every configured archive,
not only the archive the package came from.  This chapter describes the
search, the allowlist, and the guarantees before and during an
install.

@menu
* Search Order::             Which archive answers a requirement.
* Dependency Allowlist::     Every name, at every depth.
* Preflight and Atomicity::  All checks before any install.
* Core-Provided Packages::   Requirements the core satisfies.
* Cycles::                   Repeated requirements and floors.
@end menu

@node Search Order, Dependency Allowlist, Cross-Archive Dependencies, Cross-Archive Dependencies
@section Search Order

A dependency requirement names a package and may give a version floor.
The client searches every archive in @code{elpa-archives} order and
takes the first entry whose version is at or above the floor.  The
dependent's own archive does not constrain the search, so a MELPA
package can require a package from GNU ELPA.  A duplicate name in two
archives resolves to the earlier archive that satisfies the floor.

Top-level installs keep the milestone-1 rule: @code{elpa-install}
selects from the first configured archive, with an exact version when
one is given.  Only dependency requirements search the whole list.

Refresh is lazy and per consulted archive: the first time resolution
consults an archive during an install operation, that archive's index
is refreshed; later consultations reuse it.  A top-level install still
refreshes the first archive before resolving.  There is no
time-to-live and no background refresh.

@node Dependency Allowlist, Preflight and Atomicity, Search Order, Cross-Archive Dependencies
@section Dependency Allowlist

Every dependency name that is not core-provided (and is not the
@code{emacs} pseudo-dependency), at every depth, must be in
@code{elpa-installable-packages}.  There is no automatic closure of the
allowlist and no implicit trust: a dependency edge cannot introduce a
name the user has not already allowed.  A name outside the list fails
before its artifact is downloaded.  Core-provided names and the
@code{emacs} pseudo-dependency are handled separately
(@pxref{Core-Provided Packages}).

The trust model is otherwise unchanged: HTTPS transport and the flat
allowlist remain the only controls (@pxref{Security}).

@node Preflight and Atomicity, Core-Provided Packages, Dependency Allowlist, Cross-Archive Dependencies
@section Preflight and Atomicity

The whole required closure is preflighted before anything installs.
Every dependency name is checked against the allowlist; every
requirement not already satisfied by an installed build must be found
in some archive at or above its floor.  A missing dependency, an
unsatisfiable floor, a disallowed name, or a conflicting cycle floor
raises one unified dependency error naming the offending package and
the package that required it.

After a green preflight, dependencies install in dependency-first
order, then the dependent.  If any install fails, the dependent aborts
and that install's staging residue is removed.  Dependencies installed
before the failure may remain; there is no best-effort retry and no
second failure mode.

@node Core-Provided Packages, Cycles, Preflight and Atomicity, Cross-Archive Dependencies
@section Core-Provided Packages

The private constant @code{elpa-core-provided-packages} defaults to
@code{(cl cl-extra cl-lib)}.  A requirement whose name is in this list
is satisfied without a download.  The constant is internal, not a
Customize option.

The @code{emacs} pseudo-dependency is handled separately: it is
ignored, and a successful install emits one summary warning listing
the ignored @code{emacs} requirements.

@node Cycles, , Core-Provided Packages, Cross-Archive Dependencies
@section Cycles

Dependency cycles are detected while planning.  A back edge is
floor-checked against the version already planned for the cycle member
and warned, rather than silently recursed.  If the back edge demands a
version above the planned one, the install fails with the unified
dependency error.  A cycle whose floors are satisfiable installs each
member once.

@node Install Pipeline, Transport, Cross-Archive Dependencies, Top
@chapter Install Pipeline

@code{elpa-install} runs these stages in order.  Each can fail the
operation; the failing package is not published and its staging
residue is removed, though dependencies completed before a failure may
remain:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Refresh.}  Fetch, validate, cache, and parse the selected
archive's index.  A rolling archive is never trusted from cache: old
artifacts 404, so a stale index can name a dead download.  A local
directory refresh is a file read, not a network fetch.

@item
@strong{Resolve.}  Apply the allowlist to the top-level package and
pick its version from the first archive: exact match when requested
and still advertised, otherwise the version the index currently
advertises.  An explicit version that the index no longer offers fails
clearly.

@item
@strong{Preflight.}  Plan the whole required closure before anything
installs: check every dependency name against the allowlist, and
resolve every requirement not already satisfied on disk across all
archives.  Cycles are floor-checked and warned
(@pxref{Cross-Archive Dependencies}).  The plan is dependency-first;
the remaining stages run for each planned package in that order.

@item
@strong{Acquire.}  HTTPS archives are fetched over the async HTTP
transport (@pxref{Transport}) with the bytes written straight to a
staging file by C; local archives are copied.  Timeouts: 60@tie{}s for
the index, 300@tie{}s for the artifact; redirects are capped at 10.

@item
@strong{Stage.}  For @code{tar}: list the members, validate them
(@ref{Security}), extract with external @command{tar}.  For
@code{single}: create the content directory and write the file as
@file{<name>.el}, rejecting empty files that do not look like the
named library.

@item
@strong{Validate metadata.}  For @code{tar}, @file{<name>-pkg.el} must
exist and its @code{define-package} form must agree with the index on
name and version.

@item
@strong{Generate autoloads.}  Scan every @file{.el} in the content
directory for @code{;;;###autoload} cookies and write
@file{<name>-autoloads.el} whose first form adds the content directory
to @code{load-path}.  Shipped autoloads files are removed, not used.

@item
@strong{Byte-compile.}  Compile every @file{.el} in the content
directory, including the generated autoloads.  Warnings are logged and
tolerated; a compile error fails the install.

@item
@strong{Publish and replace.}  Rename the staged directory to its final
@code{<name>-<version>/} location and delete the downloaded artifact.
If an older build of the same package is installed, delete it only
after the new directory is published.  A failure before this point
leaves the old build intact; after success exactly one content
directory remains.
@end enumerate

@node Transport, Testing, Install Pipeline, Top
@chapter Transport

Downloads ride the @code{async-http} transport built on libcurl
(@pxref{Async Programming, , , internals, XEmacs Internals Manual}).
Two properties matter to the package client:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Binary safety.}  Response bodies accumulate in external binary
strings (@code{make_extstring} with @code{Qbinary}); bytes invalid in
the internal Mule encoding pass through unchanged.  Package artifacts
are additionally never accumulated in memory at all: the file
destination writes each chunk to disk inside C.

@item
@strong{Bounded failure.}  Redirect chains are capped at 10 and every
connection has a finite connect timeout (10@tie{}s unless the caller
sets a shorter total timeout), so a bad archive URL fails promptly
instead of hanging.
@end itemize

HTTP responses other than 200 produce a clear error naming the URL
(@code{archive not found} / @code{artifact not found}), and a failed
download leaves no partial file behind.

@node Testing, Limitations, Transport, Top
@chapter Testing

The client's behavior is pinned by CTest suites.  For milestone 2, the
@dfn{success test} has two halves: a deterministic offline gate that
pins exact versions, and a live smoke that pins nothing.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{elpa-tests} (offline, runs by default): the milestone-1
deterministic gate.  It installs the nine MELPA target packages from
the vendored fixture under @file{tests/elpa/melpa-fixture/}---the real,
unmodified upstream tarballs at pinned date versions plus a
hand-written version-1 @file{archive-contents}---and the GNU ELPA
targets @code{queue} and @code{loc-changes}.  For each MELPA target it
exercises one representative function in a fresh child XEmacs process,
using the forms fixed by the no-kit audit (for example,
@code{(levenshtein-distance "kitten" "sitting")} must return
@code{3}), and it covers the negative paths: allowlist rejection, an
unavailable explicit version, path-traversal and symlink members, and a
missing artifact.  Its last line is @code{elpa tests: passed}.

@item
@code{elpa-m2-tests} (offline, runs by default): the milestone-2
fixture gate.  The fixture under
@file{tests/elpa/melpa-m2-fixture/} holds two local archives: a
hand-written MELPA-style index advertising the four target tarballs at
pinned date versions, and a GNU-style index advertising @code{queue}
0.2.  The five vendored artifacts are real, unmodified upstream files;
@file{PROVENANCE} records each one's source, version, byte size, and
license.  Synthetic packages are checked in as source and tarred into
temporary archive copies at test setup, so no synthetic binary is
committed.  The gate installs each target into a temporary package
directory and exercises it in a fresh child XEmacs, comparing the
final output line by raw-byte @code{string=}.  Its last line is
@code{elpa-m2 tests: passed}.

@item
@code{elpa-m2-negative-tests} (offline, runs by default): the
milestone-2 negative gate, all on synthetic packages.  It first
installs the cross-archive witness @code{m2-xdep}, a MELPA-style
package that requires GNU ELPA @code{queue}; installing it must pull
@code{queue} 0.2 from the second archive.  It then covers a dependency
outside the allowlist, a dependency present in no index, an
unsatisfiable version floor, a corrupt dependency artifact, a
duplicate name in both archives (for both top-level and dependency
resolution), a rolling upgrade that keeps its GNU dependency, and
cycles, including a conflicting cycle floor.  The assertions require
one clear error, no partially-installed dependent, and no staging
residue; dependencies completed before a failure may remain.  Its last
line is @code{elpa-m2-negative tests: passed}.

@item
@code{cl-lib-tests} and @code{subr-compat-tests} (offline, run by
default): per-definition tests for the compatibility layer, including
the @code{cl-position} patch and the no-override guard, plus compile,
load, and fresh-child tests for the dumped macros.  CTest fails these
suites on the harness's @code{FAIL:} or @code{aborted} markers.

@item
@code{elpa-live-tests} (labeled @code{network}, skipped unless the
environment variable @code{ELPA_LIVE} is set): the live HTTPS smoke.
It refreshes the full MELPA index in memory and the GNU ELPA index,
installs @code{checkbox} from MELPA and @code{queue} from GNU ELPA
without pinning versions, asserts that each installed directory name
matches the version its index advertised, and exercises each in a
fresh child XEmacs.  It also keeps the 404 index and 404 artifact
negatives.  The live suite exercises top-level archive selection, not
a transitive dependency path; the transitive resolver is proven
offline.
@end itemize

Run them with:

@example
ctest --test-dir build -R 'elpa|cl-lib-tests|subr-compat-tests'
ELPA_LIVE=1 ctest --test-dir build -R elpa-live
@end example

@node Limitations, Concept Index, Testing, Top
@chapter Limitations

Deliberately not supported yet; none of this is claimed to work:

@itemize @bullet
@item Signature and checksum verification (see @ref{Security}); the v1
index carries no checksums, and TLS pinning is absent too.
@item A package menu, browsing, deletion, or rollback; interactive
commands beyond the documented entry points.  Re-running install
replaces an older build in place, but there is no separate upgrade
command or retention policy.
@item Packages outside @code{elpa-installable-packages}.
@item Building packages from MELPA recipes or source (VC) repositories;
the client consumes prebuilt tarballs only.
@item Emacs APIs outside the support contract (@pxref{Excluded APIs}).
@item Lexical binding.  @code{unidecode} declares
@code{lexical-binding: t} but runs unchanged because it does not depend
on lexical semantics; @code{memoize} is the documented negative, since
it closes over a @code{let}-bound hash table and needs interpreter-level
lexical binding.
@item The interactive paths of @code{loc-changes} that need
@code{insert-text-button} and friends.
@item Windows and non-GNU @command{tar} variants.
@end itemize

